{"hash":"db173d530e380c58","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":78.165,"said":"You have had your darshan, and now the path leads out through the Palki Hall, the hall of the palanquin. Remember what you heard in the queue, that Ujjain calls Mahakal its king? Here that love becomes a celebration. On every Monday from the start of the month of Shravan until Bhadrapada Amavasya, the king leaves his temple to see his people. His silver face, the Rajat Mukharavind, is seated in a silver palanquin, and the Mahakal Sawari winds through the old lanes of the city down to Ramghat on the Kshipra, where he is worshipped with the river’s water. Bands play, bhajan groups sing, and great crowds line every street. On the last Monday comes the grandest procession of all, the Shahi Sawari, on a longer route, with the Lord shown in many forms. ‘The king is going out to see how his people are,’ devotees say. He rides out again on Vijayadashami, when his procession symbolically crosses the city’s boundary to bless Ujjain. But every kingdom has a story of how it was once saved. Step out into the courtyard, and look up.\n\nDid you know? During Shiv Navratri in Phalgun, devotees see Mahakal in five forms at once, in the Panch Mukharavind darshan.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1034},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1050,"end":1160}],"words":[[0.098,0.192,0,3],[0.302,0.204,4,8],[0.519,0.252,9,12],[0.783,0.12,13,17],[0.915,0.541,18,25],[1.673,0.18,27,30],[1.865,0.204,31,34],[2.081,0.108,35,38],[2.201,0.361,39,43],[2.574,0.337,44,49],[2.923,0.252,50,53],[3.187,0.18,54,61],[3.379,0.096,62,65],[3.487,0.433,66,71],[3.932,0.373,72,76],[4.557,0.12,78,81],[4.689,0.24,82,86],[4.942,0.108,87,89],[5.062,0.096,90,93],[5.17,0.757,94,103],[6.839,0.589,105,113],[7.44,0.216,114,118],[7.668,0.12,119,122],[7.801,0.228,123,128],[8.041,0.096,129,131],[8.149,0.084,132,135],[8.245,0.349,136,141],[8.81,0.216,143,147],[9.038,0.421,148,154],[9.471,0.349,155,160],[9.832,0.493,161,168],[10.